  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-BGD301</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Policies and programmes operationalised to ensure comprehensive and sustainable social protection for all</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh has made notable socioeconomic progress and its anticipated graduation from Least Developed Country (LDC) status by 2026, yet the country continues to face major labour market and social protection challenges, including high informality, low female labour force participation, youth unemployment, and inadequate income security for workers. Although the National Social Security Strategy (NSSS) introduced in 2015 established a framework for life-cycle social protection, contributory social insurance systems for working-age populations remain limited. Existing employer-liability approaches to employment injury protection fall short of international labour standards, while protections for unemployment, maternity, and sickness remain underdeveloped.
The ILO supports the Government of Bangladesh in strengthening inclusive and sustainable social protection systems through policy reform, institutional development, and tripartite dialogue. Building on the Employment Injury Scheme (EIS) pilot introduced in the ready-made garment sector and gradually entering into the leather and ship recycling sectors, the ILO is supporting the transition toward a nationally institutionalized Employment Injury Insurance (EII) system aligned with international standards. This includes support for legal reforms, actuarial analysis, institutional assessments, and stakeholder engagement to establish a National EIS through revised Bangladesh Labour Rules and a dedicated EIS institution under government leadership.
The ILO also supports integration of a National Social Insurance Strategy roadmap into the next phase of the National Social Protection Strategy to expand social protection coverage. Through tripartite consultations and policy dialogue, the programme promotes development of unemployment, maternity, and sickness protection mechanisms, while advancing disability-inclusive social protection policies and data systems aligned with international labour standards.</narrative>
  </description>
